// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/syscall/handlers/proc.vuma
// VWK Syscall Handlers — Process (getpid, exit) (PMT-pure).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the process-control syscall-handler layer. It implements
// sys_getpid (asm-generic __NR_getpid = 172) and sys_exit (asm-generic
// __NR_exit = 93; Linux also has __NR_exit_group = 94 for exiting all
// threads in a thread group — this file's sys_exit calls the host `exit`
// which is sys_exit_group on x86_64, the standard "exit the whole process"
// path). Sibling io.vuma implements sys_write / sys_read; sibling mm.vuma
// implements sys_brk / sys_mmap / sys_munmap.
//
// ── Why direct extern dispatch (not the SyscallTable indirection)? ──────────
// Same rationale as io.vuma's header: K3e's dispatch.vuma uses a stub
// return because VUMA has no fn-pointer call today. K11 will register
// sys_getpid / sys_exit via syscall_register and wire up real dispatch.
// For now, this file is self-testable: main() calls sys_getpid directly
// to verify the path works end-to-end (sys_exit is exercised via a
// probe in /home/z/probes/probe_proc_exit.vuma — verified exit=0).
//
// ── Hosted vs bare-metal swap ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// In hosted mode (compile_dump on x86_64), the `exit` and `getpid` externs
// resolve to the pre-registered runtime stubs in
// src/codegen/src/x86_64/mod.rs::build_runtime_syscall_stubs:
//   - exit      = sys_exit_group (syscall #231 on x86_64; the simple_stub
//                 "mov eax,#231; syscall; ret" — note this is the canonical
//                 "exit the whole process" path, NOT sys_exit (60) which
//                 only exits the current thread)
//   - getpid    = sys_getpid (syscall #39 on x86_64)
// In bare-metal mode (K11), exit will become a halt-loop (e.g.
// `cli; hlt; jmp -2` on x86_64, `wfi` on aarch64/riscv64) and getpid will
// read from a per-CPU current-task struct. The handler signatures stay
// byte-identical across modes — only the extern block is the swap point.
//
// ── Why does sys_exit have a `return;` after exit(code)? ────────────────────
// VUMA's type system requires all paths through a function to return a
// value matching the declared return type. sys_exit is declared `fn
// sys_exit(code: i64)` (i.e. return type is unit/void — written as no
// `-> T`). The `exit(code)` call is declared as `fn exit(code: i64)` in
// the extern block, with no return value (it never returns — the syscall
// terminates the process). However, VUMA's parser doesn't model
// "divergent functions" (functions that never return), so the call site
// is treated as a regular call that DOES return. To satisfy the type
// checker, the function body must end with an explicit `return;` — even
// though that line is unreachable. Same pattern as any VUMA fn that
// calls a divergent extern (e.g. K11's panic handler will have the same
// shape: `panic(msg); return;`).
//
// ── Why does sys_getpid return i64, not u64? ───────────────────────────────
// Linux's getpid returns pid_t (a positive int — process IDs are 1..4194304
// on x86_64 Linux, well within int range). The host `getpid` extern is
// declared `fn getpid() -> i64` (matches the C signature `pid_t getpid(void)`
// where pid_t is `int`, sign-extended to 64 bits in the SysV AMD64 ABI).
// sys_getpid passes the i64 straight through. The CALLER (K3e's
// syscall_write_ret) writes the i64 return value into tf.rax (a u64 field
// — VUMA's i64/u64 implicit-conversion handles the bit-pattern identity).
//
// ── Why is there no sys_exit_group wrapper? ────────────────────────────────
// Linux has BOTH sys_exit (60) and sys_exit_group (94) on x86_64. sys_exit
// exits only the calling thread; sys_exit_group exits the whole thread
// group (the canonical "exit the process" path). This file's `exit` extern
// resolves to sys_exit_group (231 in the runtime stub table — see the
// syscall #231 entry in build_runtime_syscall_stubs). The handler name is
// `sys_exit` (matching the asm-generic __NR_exit = 93 contract), but the
// underlying call is exit_group semantics. K11 may add a separate
// sys_exit_thread handler if real thread support lands; for now, sys_exit
// = exit-the-whole-process is the only path.
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The
// handlers take only scalar args (i64) and return scalars — no State<T>
// buffers needed. No state_new, no array access, no field access.
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL per the K3f contract's
// "IMPORTANT" rule (none are used in this file's source — the syscall
// numbers 172/93/94 are noted in the header comments but not used as
// literals; the extern dispatch is by NAME, not by number, on the host
// side. The asm-generic numbers are only used by K3e's dispatch.vuma to
// index into SyscallTable — they live in kmain's
// syscall_register(tbl, 172, &sys_getpid) call, K11's job to wire up).
// Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix width-
// extension subtleties — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for
// the same rationale).
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. The `extern "C" { fn exit, fn getpid }`
// declarations are byte-identical to:
//   - womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/trampoline.vuma (K1a)
//   - womb/kernel/hosted/host.vuma (K1a — host_exit wraps exit)
// Future files that want to call sys_getpid / sys_exit (e.g. K4's kmain
// when it bootstraps the syscall table) must either re-declare these
// handlers locally OR wait for VUMA to gain an import mechanism.
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/syscall/handlers/proc.vuma \
//         /tmp/proc.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/proc.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (getpid > 0 — we're a running process).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Extern declarations (byte-identical to K1a's trampoline.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Hosted mode: exit = sys_exit_group (syscall #231 on x86_64), getpid =
// sys_getpid (syscall #39 on x86_64). Both use the simple_stub form
// ("mov eax,#num; syscall; ret") — no register shuffling needed (exit
// takes 1 arg in RDI; getpid takes 0 args). exit never returns (the
// syscall terminates the process); getpid returns the PID in RAX.

extern "C" {
    fn exit(code: i64);
    fn getpid() -> i64;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// sys_getpid — return the current process ID.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// In hosted mode, calls the host getpid. The returned PID is a positive
// i64 (1..4194304 on x86_64 Linux). In bare-metal mode (K11), this will
// read from a per-CPU current-task struct (K4's scheduler will maintain
// it).

fn sys_getpid() -> i64 {
    return getpid();
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// sys_exit — terminate the current process with `code`.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// In hosted mode, calls the host exit (sys_exit_group on x86_64) — the
// process terminates with exit code `code`. In bare-metal mode (K11),
// this will become a halt-loop (`cli; hlt; jmp -2` on x86_64). The
// `return;` after the exit call is unreachable but required by VUMA's
// type checker (see "Why does sys_exit have a `return;` after exit(code)?"
// in the file header).

fn sys_exit(code: i64) {
    exit(code);
    return;  // unreachable, but VUMA needs a return
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Call sys_getpid and verify the returned PID is > 0 (we're a running
// process — the host kernel assigned us a positive PID). Returns 0 on
// success, 1 on PID-not-positive failure.
//
// sys_exit is NOT exercised in the self-test because calling it would
// terminate the test process before the test framework could capture
// the exit code. (A separate probe at /home/z/probes/probe_proc_exit.vuma
// verifies sys_exit(0) actually exits with code 0.) sys_exit's compile-
// time correctness is verified by the IVE Pass + the file's parse success.
//
// Per the K3f contract's "IMPORTANT" rule: all non-zero constants in the
// self-test use DECIMAL (none — the `0` comparison is the only constant,
// and it's the natural zero) — decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal
// path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties — see womb/kernel/
// arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale).

fn main() -> i32 {
    let pid = sys_getpid();
    // PID should be > 0 (we're a running process).
    if pid <= 0 { return 1; }
    return 0;
}
